\begin_layout Part*
Overview of the algorithm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These are the main steps of the forward warping LF recontruction algorithm:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We find the locations on the phase mask which are of interest to us.
 This means the locations that LF camera intersects with, using the angles
 and locations 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We interpolate the values of the phase mask gradient in these locations,
 based on the given phase mask
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We find the FW locations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We create a transformation matrix, in order to apply the forwarding
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We perform the forward warping using the transformation matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Definitions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to help the explanation be clear, we will define several things.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
LF
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, a lightfield LF has 4 variables:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X,Y,k_{x},k_{y}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is constant, we can define 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{x}=\sin\theta_{x},k_{x}=\sin\theta_{y}$
\end_inset

, ignoring the constant 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 angle is defind between the ray (after projecting to 2d slice) and the
 z axis (the optical axis).
 A light ray coming from the right (going left) will have a positive 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 This is how it seems to be defined in the LF object.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, a lightfield space has 4 variables...
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The distance between the LF and the phase mask is 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Phase Mask
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Phase mask can be seen as a 2D function that has 2 variables:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X,Y
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function returns a change in 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta k_{x},\Delta k_{y}$
\end_inset

.We will notate this function as 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{\phi}\left(x,y\right)$
\end_inset

.
 We will define the change as thus:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If a ray passes the Phase Mask with momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{k}$
\end_inset

 the output ray will have momentum 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{k}+\Delta\vec{k}$
\end_inset

 before normalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
2 Different LFs Spaces
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have 2 different LF spaces.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, the one that was input.
 We will notate this by the letter 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Second, we have the LF we will reconstruct, which will be notated by the
 letter 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us go over the algorithm steps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Algorithm Steps
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each location on 
\begin_inset Formula $LF$
\end_inset

 (for now it doesn't matter which LF, as they have the same axes) we find
 where the ray intersects with the phase mask.
 At location 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x,y,k_{x},k_{y}\right)$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $LF$
\end_inset

 the intersecting point will be:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(x-\Delta x,y-\Delta y\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta x=-L\tan\theta_{x}=-L\frac{\sin\theta_{x}}{\cos\theta_{x}}=-L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{PM}\left(x,y,k_{x},k_{y}\right)=x+L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{PM}\left(x,y,k_{x},k_{y}\right)=y+L\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we interpolate the change in 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 due to the phase mask at this location:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\vec{\Delta k}=\vec{\phi}\left(x_{PM},y_{PM}\right)=\vec{\phi}\left(x+L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}},y+L\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we find the FW location for each LF location.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, the new location is at:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{FW}=x-\Delta x+\Delta x_{new}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta x=-L\tan\theta_{x}=-L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta x_{new}=-L\cdot\tan\theta_{x,new}=-L\cdot\frac{k_{x,new}}{k_{z,new}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{x,new}=k_{x}-\Delta k_{x}\left(x+L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}},y+L\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
k_{y,new}=k_{y}-\Delta k_{y}\left(x+L\frac{k_{x}}{k_{z}},y+L\frac{k_{y}}{k_{z}}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have now found, for each location of the LF, the forward location it
 must go to.
 However, when doing forward warping we must be careful in our summation
 with out weights.
 As several pixels in the Input LF can contribute to the Reconstructed LF.
 Furthermore, each pixel in the Input LF, can will contribute to 4 (almost
 always) pixels in the Reconstructed LF.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This transformation can be described using a matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We flatten the LF matrix to get 
\begin_inset Formula $LF_{I}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to convert this to 
\begin_inset Formula $LF_{R}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we need a transformation matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
LF_{R}=M\cdot LF_{I}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice the sizes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(LF_{I}\right)_{\left|X_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|Y_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{x,LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{y,LF}\right|}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(LF_{R}\right)_{\left|X_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|Y_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{x,LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{y,LF}\right|}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
M_{\left|X_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|Y_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{x,LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{y,LF}\right|,\left|X_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|Y_{LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{x,LF}\right|\cdot\left|\theta_{y,LF}\right|}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to create 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 we do the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find for each point 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x_{FW},y_{FW}\right)$
\end_inset

 the 4 relevant indices in the Reconstructed LF grid they fall in between.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Test if these indices are valid, as in, are they in the grid.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Calculate the weights for each of these 4 pixels.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Convert these indices to indices for the flattened Reconstructed LF
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Define the relevant rows in the 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 matrix.
 The relevant rows are the relevant indices we have found.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Define the relevant columns, which just make sure that we take each point
 4, as mentioned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Create a Sparse matrix, with these rows, columns, and weights as defined
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we can we can perform the forward warping:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
LF_{r}\left(x,y,k_{x},k_{y}\right)=W\cdot LF_{i}\left(x,y,k_{x},k_{y}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

